\[x' = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}\] <p>where \(\mu = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\) and \(\sigma = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2}\).</p> <p>Effect: Transforms the feature to have zero mean and unit variance. The transformed distribution is not necessarily Gaussian — it depends on the original distribution. What you get is a feature centred at 0 with spread measured in standard deviations.</p> <p>When to use: The default for most algorithms — SVMs, KNN, PCA, neural networks, logistic regression with regularisation.</p> <p>What it assumes: The mean and variance are meaningful summary statistics. This holds for roughly unimodal, symmetric distributions. For heavy-tailed or multimodal distributions, it can still be done, but interpret with care.</p> <p>Critical leakage rule: Compute \(\mu\) and \(\sigma\) only on the training set. Apply the same parameters to the validation and test sets. Computing on the full dataset leaks test information into training.</p> <pre><code>import numpy as np

class StandardScaler:
    """Minimal implementation to understand what sklearn does."""
    def fit(self, X):
        self.mean_ = X.mean(axis=0)
        self.std_  = X.std(axis=0)
        return self

    def transform(self, X):
        return (X - self.mean_) / (self.std_ + 1e-8)  # epsilon avoids div-by-zero

    def fit_transform(self, X):
        return self.fit(X).transform(X)

# Usage pattern that prevents leakage:
scaler = StandardScaler()
X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)  # fit AND transform on train
X_test_scaled  = scaler.transform(X_test)        # ONLY transform on test
</code></pre>

\[x' = \frac{x - x_{\min}}{x_{\max} - x_{\min}}\] <p>Effect: Transforms values to \([0, 1]\). The zero of the original scale maps to 0; the maximum maps to 1.</p> <p>When to use:</p> <ul> <li>When you need bounded output (e.g., feeding into sigmoid activations).</li> <li>When the algorithm is sensitive to magnitude but you want to preserve exact zero (unlike standardisation, which shifts the mean away from zero).</li> <li>Image pixel values: divide by 255 to get \([0,1]\).</li> </ul> <p>Vulnerability: Extremely sensitive to outliers. A single outlier at \(x = 10^6\) compresses everything else near 0. This is its main weakness compared to standardisation.</p> <pre><code>class MinMaxScaler:
    def fit(self, X):
        self.min_ = X.min(axis=0)
        self.max_ = X.max(axis=0)
        return self

    def transform(self, X):
        return (X - self.min_) / (self.max_ - self.min_ + 1e-8)
</code></pre>

\[x' = \frac{x - \text{median}(x)}{\text{IQR}(x)}\] <p>where \(\text{IQR}(x) = Q_3(x) - Q_1(x)\), the interquartile range (75<sup>th</sup> percentile minus 25<sup>th</sup> percentile).</p> <p>Effect: Like standardisation but using robust statistics. Outliers have little influence on the median and IQR, so the transformation is stable even when the feature has heavy tails or extreme values.</p> <p>When to use: When your feature has outliers that you cannot or do not want to remove — financial data, medical measurements, sensor readings.</p> <pre><code>class RobustScaler:
    def fit(self, X):
        self.median_ = np.median(X, axis=0)
        self.iqr_    = np.percentile(X, 75, axis=0) - np.percentile(X, 25, axis=0)
        return self

    def transform(self, X):
        return (X - self.median_) / (self.iqr_ + 1e-8)
</code></pre>
